/**
Bindings to ENet, reliable UDP networking for games and real-time apps.

enetxt wraps ENet 1.3.18. A host is a socket: a server host listens on a
port, a client host only connects out. A peer is one connection on a host.
Hosts and peers are integer handles; 0 means the call failed (see
<enLastError>), and a handle that has gone stale is a harmless no-op.

ENet has no threads of its own. Nothing connects, sends or receives until
<enPoll> services the host, so call it on a timer for as long as a host
lives. The enetHelpers library (enStartPolling) does that for you and
dispatches each event as a message: enetConnect, enetDisconnect,
enetReceive and enetError.

Call <enInitialize> once at startup and <enDeinitialize> once at quit.

Tags: Networking, Library
*/
library org.openxtalk.library.enet

-- enet.lcb - the LCB binding for enetxt (library org.openxtalk.library.enet).
-- It declares one `private foreign handler` per enx_* symbol in src/enx_abi.h
-- and wraps each in a `public en*` handler that hides every handle, pre-sizes
-- and reuses the poll/scratch buffers, walks the big-endian KV records by
-- byte arithmetic, and never throws into script.
--
-- THE MODEL (the one ENet fact an xTalk author must know): PUMP OR NOTHING.
-- ENet has no threads of its own - nothing connects, sends, or receives until
-- enPoll services the host. Run enPoll on a timer (the helpers stack does it
-- for you); its interval is the latency floor - 16-33 ms feels real-time,
-- 100+ ms is fine for chat. Delivery of QUEUED sends also rides the pump (or
-- an explicit enFlush).
--
-- The FFI idiom for byte buffers (carried from TorrentXT - the hard-won
-- lesson). An LCB Data does NOT auto-bridge to a C `void*` (the Language
-- Reference is explicit: "No automatic bridging from Data or String to
-- Pointer exists" - a Data marshals as an MCDataRef). So:
--   * OUTPUT buffers (C fills them) are raw blocks from the engine <builtin>
--     MCMemoryAllocate, passed as a real Pointer. C returns bytes-written (or
--     the negative of the capacity it needed, so we grow and retry), then we
--     copy the written bytes back into a Data with MCDataCreateWithBytes and
--     walk them.
--   * INPUT buffers (C reads them) pass MCDataGetBytePtr(theData) - the
--     read-only pointer to a Data's own bytes - together with the byte count.
--   * records are walked with `the code of (byte I of D)` (big-endian u16)
--     and `byte X to Y of D` for sub-ranges.
--   * every foreign call sits inside `unsafe ... end unsafe`; all `variable`s
--     are declared at the top of their handler.
--
-- The numeric constants below MIRROR src/enx_record.h; tools/check-record-
-- registry.py proves they never drift.
--
-- OXT cannot compile/run .lcb headlessly, so every edit gets the static gate
-- (tools/check-livecodescript.py) and runtime confirmation waits on a manual
-- OXT pass. The selftest suite ran green in OXT on 2026-08-07 (the async
-- loopback standalone 2026-08-13; the sync half folded in the suite paste,
-- most recently 2026-09-25 on Linux, where the paste's own live loopback
-- completed too, and on Windows, where it stalled again: CLAUDE.md's
-- ledger).

use com.livecode.foreign

metadata version is "0.1.0"
metadata author is "Seth Morrow Software"
metadata title is "enetxt"

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Constants
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Must equal ENX_ABI_VERSION in src/enx_abi.h; _checkABI() throws on skew.
constant kABIVersion is 2

-- libfoundation MCStringEncoding value for UTF-8 (ASCII=0, Windows1252=1,
-- MacRoman=2, ISO8859_1=3, UTF8=4). Used by _decodeText / _encodeText.
constant kEncodingUtf8 is 4

-- The per-message size budget, mirroring ENX_MAX_MESSAGE in enx_abi.h: sends
-- above it fail with error -4 (too large), never truncated. Packets are
-- MESSAGES (game state, chat, control); bulk belongs to TorrentXT.
constant kMaxMessage is 60000

-- Reusable buffer capacities (the single-thread performance playbook:
-- allocate once, reuse every poll, never rebuild). Grown on demand when the
-- shim reports a bigger -needed. The drain default holds one max-size packet
-- with room to spare; scratch covers version/error strings and status records.
constant kDrainCap is 65536
constant kScratchCap is 2048

-- Record field value types (mirror enx_record.h FieldType -> kType*).
constant kTypeInt is 0
constant kTypeReal is 1
constant kTypeUtf8 is 2
constant kTypeRaw is 3
constant kTypeHex is 4

-- Record field ids (mirror enx_record.h FieldId -> kField*).
constant kFieldEnHost is 1
constant kFieldEnPeer is 2
constant kFieldEnChannel is 3
constant kFieldEnPayload is 4
constant kFieldEnData is 5
constant kFieldEnAddress is 6
constant kFieldEnState is 7
constant kFieldEnRtt is 8
constant kFieldEnPacketLoss is 9
constant kFieldEnPacketsSent is 10
constant kFieldEnPacketsLost is 11
constant kFieldEnBytesSent is 12
constant kFieldEnBytesReceived is 13
constant kFieldEnPeerCount is 14
constant kFieldEnError is 15

-- Stable event type codes (mirror enx_record.h EventType -> kEvent*).
constant kEventConnect is 1
constant kEventDisconnect is 2
constant kEventReceive is 3
constant kEventError is 4

-- ENet peer states (mirror enx_record.h PeerState -> kPeerState*; ENet's own
-- numbering, static_asserted in the shim). enPeerStatus's "state" field is
-- one of these. Most scripts only branch on Connected / Disconnected.
constant kPeerStateDisconnected is 0
constant kPeerStateConnecting is 1
constant kPeerStateAcknowledgingConnect is 2
constant kPeerStateConnectionPending is 3
constant kPeerStateConnectionSucceeded is 4
constant kPeerStateConnected is 5
constant kPeerStateDisconnectLater is 6
constant kPeerStateDisconnecting is 7
constant kPeerStateAcknowledgeDisconnect is 8
constant kPeerStateZombie is 9

-- Delivery modes for enSend/enBroadcast (mirror enx_record.h SendFlag ->
-- kSend*). Reliable is the safe default; Unreliable is sequenced-but-
-- droppable (stale-able state updates); Unsequenced is fire-and-forget.
constant kSendReliable is 0
constant kSendUnreliable is 1
constant kSendUnsequenced is 2

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Module state: the reusable poll/scratch buffers
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Reusable raw buffers for the fill-a-buffer FFI (allocated via the verified
-- engine <builtin> MCMemoryAllocate; an LCB Data does not bridge to a void*).
-- Each pairs a Pointer with its current byte capacity. They persist for the
-- process - bounded reuse, the single-thread playbook - because there is no
-- deterministic library-unload hook to free them at.
variable sDrainPtr as Pointer
variable sDrainCap as Integer
variable sScratchPtr as Pointer
variable sScratchCap as Integer

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Foreign handlers (one per enx_* symbol in enx_abi.h). Buffer params are
--  `Pointer` - we hand them MCMemoryAllocate blocks (output) or
--  MCDataGetBytePtr pointers (input), never a Data value; short strings are
--  ZStringUTF8. All cdecl. Never rename these binds.
-- ===================================================================== --

private foreign handler _enx_abi_version() returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_abi_version!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_initialize() returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_initialize!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_deinitialize() returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_deinitialize!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_version_string(in pOut as Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_version_string!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_last_error(in pOut as Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_last_error!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_clear_error() returns nothing binds to "c:enetxt>enx_clear_error!cdecl"

private foreign handler _enx_host_create_server(in pBindHost as ZStringUTF8, in pPort as CInt, in pMaxPeers as CInt, in pChannels as CInt, in pInBW as CInt, in pOutBW as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_host_create_server!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_host_create_client(in pMaxPeers as CInt, in pChannels as CInt, in pInBW as CInt, in pOutBW as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_host_create_client!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_host_destroy(in pHost as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_host_destroy!cdecl"

private foreign handler _enx_connect(in pHost as CInt, in pRemoteHost as ZStringUTF8, in pPort as CInt, in pChannels as CInt, in pData as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_connect!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_disconnect(in pPeer as CInt, in pData as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_disconnect!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_disconnect_now(in pPeer as CInt, in pData as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_disconnect_now!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_reset_peer(in pPeer as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_reset_peer!cdecl"

private foreign handler _enx_send(in pPeer as CInt, in pChannel as CInt, in pData as Pointer, in pLen as CInt, in pFlags as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_send!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_broadcast(in pHost as CInt, in pChannel as CInt, in pData as Pointer, in pLen as CInt, in pFlags as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_broadcast!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_flush(in pHost as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_flush!cdecl"

private foreign handler _enx_set_peer_timeout(in pPeer as CInt, in pLimit as CInt, in pMinMs as CInt, in pMaxMs as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_set_peer_timeout!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_set_peer_ping_interval(in pPeer as CInt, in pMs as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_set_peer_ping_interval!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_set_host_bandwidth(in pHost as CInt, in pInBW as CInt, in pOutBW as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_set_host_bandwidth!cdecl"

private foreign handler _enx_peer_status(in pPeer as CInt, in pOut as Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_peer_status!cdecl"
private foreign handler _enx_host_status(in pHost as CInt, in pOut as Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_host_status!cdecl"

private foreign handler _enx_poll(in pHost as CInt, in pOut as Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:enetxt>enx_poll!cdecl"

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Engine Foundation <builtin> foreign handlers
-- ===================================================================== --
-- These bind to the engine's own libfoundation, not to our shim. They are the
-- sanctioned bridge between LCB Data/Pointer/String and raw C buffers. NOTE:
-- a `<builtin>` handler resolves by its NAME matching the engine symbol, so
-- these - unlike every other private handler in this file - carry NO leading
-- `_`. Renaming any of them breaks the binding.
private foreign handler MCStringDecode(in pData as Data, in pEncoding as CInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCStringEncode(in pString as String, in pEncoding as CInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCMemoryAllocate(in pSize as UIntSize, out rBlock as Pointer) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCMemoryDeallocate(in pBlock as Pointer) returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCDataCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pCount as CUInt, out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCDataGetBytePtr(in pData as Data) returns Pointer binds to "<builtin>"

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Private helpers
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Throw a clear error if the loaded native library's ABI does not match this
-- binding. Better a legible throw at startup than a crash at first use.
private handler _checkABI()
   variable tABI as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_abi_version() into tABI
   end unsafe
   if tABI is not kABIVersion then
      throw "enetxt native library ABI does not match the LCB binding; rebuild and repackage the native library."
   end if
end handler

-- Ensure the reusable drain buffer holds at least pNeed bytes. A grow frees
-- the old block and allocates fresh (MCMemoryReallocate is not a confirmed
-- <builtin>); it only happens when the shim reports a -needed past the
-- current capacity, which is rare. Capacity 0 doubles as "not allocated yet".
private handler _ensureDrain(in pNeed as Integer)
   variable tOk as Boolean
   if sDrainCap < pNeed then
      if sDrainCap > 0 then
         unsafe
            MCMemoryDeallocate(sDrainPtr)
         end unsafe
      end if
      put false into tOk
      unsafe
         put MCMemoryAllocate(pNeed, sDrainPtr) into tOk
      end unsafe
      if not tOk then
         throw "enetxt: failed to allocate the drain buffer."
      end if
      put pNeed into sDrainCap
   end if
end handler

private handler _ensureScratch(in pNeed as Integer)
   variable tOk as Boolean
   if sScratchCap < pNeed then
      if sScratchCap > 0 then
         unsafe
            MCMemoryDeallocate(sScratchPtr)
         end unsafe
      end if
      put false into tOk
      unsafe
         put MCMemoryAllocate(pNeed, sScratchPtr) into tOk
      end unsafe
      if not tOk then
         throw "enetxt: failed to allocate the scratch buffer."
      end if
      put pNeed into sScratchCap
   end if
end handler

-- Copy the pLen bytes the shim wrote into our raw buffer back into a fresh
-- Data the record walkers can index by byte. Empty Data on a non-positive
-- length or an allocation failure.
private handler _bufToData(in pPtr as Pointer, in pLen as Integer) returns Data
   variable tData as Data
   variable tOk as Boolean
   put the empty data into tData
   if pLen > 0 then
      put false into tOk
      unsafe
         put MCDataCreateWithBytes(pPtr, pLen, tData) into tOk
      end unsafe
      if not tOk then
         put the empty data into tData
      end if
   end if
   return tData
end handler

-- Read a big-endian u16 at 1-based byte offset pOffset (the wire is network
-- order; this is the inverse of the shim's put_u16).
private handler _readU16(in pData as Data, in pOffset as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tHi as Integer
   variable tLo as Integer
   put the code of (byte pOffset of pData) into tHi
   put the code of (byte (pOffset + 1) of pData) into tLo
   return tHi * 256 + tLo
end handler

-- Decode pLen bytes of UTF-8 (1-based pStart) into a String via the engine's
-- MCStringDecode (declared above); LCB has no `textDecode` handler.
private handler _decodeText(in pData as Data, in pStart as Integer, in pLen as Integer) returns String
   variable tSub as Data
   variable tStr as String
   variable tOk as Boolean
   if pLen <= 0 then
      return ""
   end if
   put byte pStart to (pStart + pLen - 1) of pData into tSub
   put "" into tStr
   unsafe
      put MCStringDecode(tSub, kEncodingUtf8, false, tStr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if tOk then
      return tStr
   end if
   return ""
end handler

-- Encode a String to UTF-8 bytes (the inverse; used by enSendText /
-- enBroadcastText so a text payload rides the same byte path as a Data one).
private handler _encodeText(in pText as String) returns Data
   variable tData as Data
   variable tOk as Boolean
   put the empty data into tData
   put false into tOk
   unsafe
      put MCStringEncode(pText, kEncodingUtf8, false, tData) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      put the empty data into tData
   end if
   return tData
end handler

-- Map a record field id to its script-facing array key. "" means "skip"
-- (an unknown id, which the registry checker prevents in practice).
private handler _fieldKey(in pId as Integer) returns String
   if pId is kFieldEnHost then
      return "host"
   else if pId is kFieldEnPeer then
      return "peer"
   else if pId is kFieldEnChannel then
      return "channel"
   else if pId is kFieldEnPayload then
      return "payload"
   else if pId is kFieldEnData then
      return "data"
   else if pId is kFieldEnAddress then
      return "address"
   else if pId is kFieldEnState then
      return "state"
   else if pId is kFieldEnRtt then
      return "rtt"
   else if pId is kFieldEnPacketLoss then
      return "packetLoss"
   else if pId is kFieldEnPacketsSent then
      return "packetsSent"
   else if pId is kFieldEnPacketsLost then
      return "packetsLost"
   else if pId is kFieldEnBytesSent then
      return "bytesSent"
   else if pId is kFieldEnBytesReceived then
      return "bytesReceived"
   else if pId is kFieldEnPeerCount then
      return "peerCount"
   else if pId is kFieldEnError then
      return "error"
   else
      return ""
   end if
end handler

-- Map a stable event code to the semantic message name the poll dispatcher
-- sends (see examples/enet-helpers.livecodescript).
private handler _eventName(in pCode as Integer) returns String
   if pCode is kEventConnect then
      return "enetConnect"
   else if pCode is kEventDisconnect then
      return "enetDisconnect"
   else if pCode is kEventReceive then
      return "enetReceive"
   else if pCode is kEventError then
      return "enetError"
   else
      return "enetEvent"
   end if
end handler

-- Walk one bare kvrecord starting at 1-based pStart into an Array, using the
-- field-id -> key mapping. Raw fields become Data; everything else a String
-- (lossless for display and `the number`-parsable for ints).
private handler _parseRecord(in pData as Data, in pStart as Integer) returns Array
   variable tRec as Array
   variable tOffset as Integer
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tI as Integer
   variable tId as Integer
   variable tType as Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tKey as String
   put {} into tRec
   put pStart into tOffset
   put _readU16(pData, tOffset) into tCount
   add 2 to tOffset
   repeat with tI from 1 up to tCount
      put the code of (byte tOffset of pData) into tId
      put the code of (byte (tOffset + 1) of pData) into tType
      add 2 to tOffset
      put _readU16(pData, tOffset) into tLen
      add 2 to tOffset
      put _fieldKey(tId) into tKey
      if tKey is not "" then
         if tType is kTypeRaw then
            if tLen > 0 then
               put byte tOffset to (tOffset + tLen - 1) of pData into tRec[tKey]
            else
               put the empty data into tRec[tKey]
            end if
         else
            put _decodeText(pData, tOffset, tLen) into tRec[tKey]
         end if
      end if
      add tLen to tOffset
   end repeat
   return tRec
end handler

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Public API: lifecycle & diagnostics
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Initialise the native layer (refcounted; extra calls are cheap no-ops).
-- Verifies the ABI first (throws a clear error on skew). Call once at
-- startup, and pair with enDeinitialize at quit. Returns 0 on success.
/**
Summary: Initialize the ENet library before any other call.

Returns: 0 on success, or a negative error.

Example:
if enInitialize() is not 0 then answer "ENet failed:" && enLastError()

Description:
Use <enInitialize> once at startup, before creating a host. It checks
that the installed native library matches this binding and throws an
error naming both versions if it does not. Calls are counted: each
<enInitialize> needs one matching <enDeinitialize>, and extra calls are
cheap.

References: enDeinitialize (handler), enLastError (handler)
*/
public handler enInitialize() returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   _checkABI()
   unsafe
      put _enx_initialize() into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- Balance enInitialize. The FINAL deinitialize also destroys every host
-- still alive (and retires its peers) so a quitting app cannot leak
-- sockets. The app MUST call this before quitting (e.g. on closeStack) -
-- there is no automatic unload hook. Idempotent. Returns 0 on success.
/**
Summary: Release the ENet library when your app is done with it.

Returns: 0 on success.

Example:
on closeStack
   enDeinitialize
end closeStack

Description:
Use <enDeinitialize> once for each <enInitialize> your stack made,
typically on closeStack. The last one destroys every host still alive and
retires its peers, so an app cannot leak sockets. There is no automatic
unload hook: an app that quits without it leaves the sockets to the
operating system.

Write it as a bare command. The count is shared by the whole process, so
a stack must give back only the holds it took: an extra call can destroy
the hosts of another window that uses ENet.

References: enInitialize (handler)
*/
public handler enDeinitialize() returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_deinitialize() into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- "enet A.B.C" - which pinned library this binary linked.
/**
Summary: The version of the ENet library that enetxt was built with.

Returns: A string such as "enet 1.3.18".

Example:
put enLibraryVersion() into field "Version"

Description:
Use <enLibraryVersion> to report which ENet the installed native library
links, for example in an about box or a bug report.
*/
public handler enLibraryVersion() returns String
   variable tCount as Integer
   _ensureScratch(kScratchCap)
   unsafe
      put _enx_version_string(sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
   end unsafe
   if tCount < 0 then
      _ensureScratch(-tCount)
      unsafe
         put _enx_version_string(sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
      end unsafe
   end if
   if tCount <= 0 then
      return ""
   end if
   return _decodeText(_bufToData(sScratchPtr, tCount), 1, tCount)
end handler

-- The module-static last-error string ("" when there is no error).
/**
Summary: The last error message from the native layer.

Returns: The message, or empty when there is no error.

Example:
local tHost
put enHostCreateServer("", 27000, 32, 2, 0, 0) into tHost
if tHost is 0 then answer "Could not listen:" && enLastError()

Description:
Use <enLastError> after a call returns 0 or a negative number to find out
why. The message stays until the next error or <enClearError>.

References: enClearError (handler)
*/
public handler enLastError() returns String
   variable tCount as Integer
   _ensureScratch(kScratchCap)
   unsafe
      put _enx_last_error(sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
   end unsafe
   if tCount < 0 then
      _ensureScratch(-tCount)
      unsafe
         put _enx_last_error(sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
      end unsafe
   end if
   if tCount <= 0 then
      return ""
   end if
   return _decodeText(_bufToData(sScratchPtr, tCount), 1, tCount)
end handler

/**
Summary: Clear the last error message.

Example:
enClearError

Description:
Use <enClearError> before a call when you want <enLastError> to report
only that call's failure.

References: enLastError (handler)
*/
public handler enClearError()
   unsafe
      _enx_clear_error()
   end unsafe
end handler

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Public API: hosts
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Bind pPort and listen. pBindHost "" (or "*") binds every interface; an IP
-- or name binds one. pMaxPeers 1..4095; pChannels 1..255 (fixed for the
-- host's life - choose deliberately); bandwidth 0 = unlimited (bytes/sec).
-- Returns a positive host handle, or 0 on failure (see enLastError).
/**
Summary: Create a host that listens for connections on a port.

pBindHost: The address to listen on: empty or "*" for every interface,
or one IP address or name.

pPort: The UDP port to listen on, or 0 for any free port.

pMaxPeers: The most peers that may connect at once, 1 to 4095.

pChannels: The number of channels each connection has, 1 to 255. It is
fixed for the host's life.

pInBW: The incoming bandwidth in bytes per second, or 0 for unlimited.

pOutBW: The outgoing bandwidth in bytes per second, or 0 for unlimited.

Returns: A positive host handle, or 0 on failure (see <enLastError>).

Example:
local tServer
put enHostCreateServer("", 27000, 32, 2, 0, 0) into tServer

Description:
Use <enHostCreateServer> to accept connections from other machines. A
server host can also connect out with <enConnect>. Peers that connect
arrive as enetConnect events from <enPoll>. With port 0, <enHostStatus>
reports the port the system chose.

References: enHostCreateClient (handler), enHostDestroy (handler),
enPoll (handler)
*/
public handler enHostCreateServer(in pBindHost as String, in pPort as Integer, in pMaxPeers as Integer, in pChannels as Integer, in pInBW as Integer, in pOutBW as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tHost as Integer
   _checkABI()
   unsafe
      put _enx_host_create_server(pBindHost, pPort, pMaxPeers, pChannels, pInBW, pOutBW) into tHost
   end unsafe
   return tHost
end handler

-- An unbound host: it can only initiate connections (enConnect). A pure
-- client wants pMaxPeers 1; a mesh node may want more.
/**
Summary: Create a host that only makes outgoing connections.

pMaxPeers: The most connections the host may make at once; a plain
client wants 1.

pChannels: The number of channels each connection has, 1 to 255.

pInBW: The incoming bandwidth in bytes per second, or 0 for unlimited.

pOutBW: The outgoing bandwidth in bytes per second, or 0 for unlimited.

Returns: A positive host handle, or 0 on failure (see <enLastError>).

Example:
local tClient, tPeer
put enHostCreateClient(1, 2, 0, 0) into tClient
put enConnect(tClient, "192.168.1.10", 27000, 2, 0) into tPeer

Description:
Use <enHostCreateClient> for the side of a game or chat that joins a
server. The host is not bound to a known port, so nobody can connect to
it.

References: enHostCreateServer (handler), enConnect (handler)
*/
public handler enHostCreateClient(in pMaxPeers as Integer, in pChannels as Integer, in pInBW as Integer, in pOutBW as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tHost as Integer
   _checkABI()
   unsafe
      put _enx_host_create_client(pMaxPeers, pChannels, pInBW, pOutBW) into tHost
   end unsafe
   return tHost
end handler

-- Destroy the host, its socket, and every peer handle riding it. Idempotent;
-- a stale handle is a successful no-op. Returns 0.
/**
Summary: Close a host and every connection on it.

pHost: The host handle.

Returns: 0.

Example:
if sHost is not empty and sHost is not 0 then
   enHostDestroy sHost
end if
put empty into sHost

Description:
Use <enHostDestroy> when you are finished with a host. Its socket closes
and every peer handle on it retires. Destroying a host twice is
harmless, but the handle must be a number: passing empty throws an
error, so guard any variable that may have been cleared, as the example
does.

References: enHostCreateServer (handler), enHostCreateClient (handler)
*/
public handler enHostDestroy(in pHost as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_host_destroy(pHost) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Public API: peers
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Begin connecting pHost to pRemoteHost:pPort. Returns the peer handle NOW
-- (optimistically); the enetConnect event confirms it, or enetDisconnect
-- reports the failure (ENet signals a timed-out connect as a disconnect).
-- pChannels must not exceed the host's channel count; pData is a u32 the far
-- side receives in ITS enetConnect event (a protocol version, a room id...).
-- NOTE: a hostname here resolves DNS synchronously and can block briefly -
-- pass an IP when that matters. 0 on failure (see enLastError).
/**
Summary: Start connecting a host to a remote host.

pHost: The local host handle.

pRemoteHost: The IP address or name of the remote host.

pPort: The remote port.

pChannels: The number of channels to open, no more than the host has.

pData: A number from 0 to 2147483647 that the remote side receives in
its enetConnect event, such as a protocol version.

Returns: A peer handle at once, or 0 on failure (see <enLastError>).

Example:
put enConnect(sClient, "192.168.1.10", 27000, 2, 1) into sPeer

Description:
Use <enConnect> to open a connection. The handle is returned before the
connection exists: an enetConnect event from <enPoll> confirms it, and an
enetDisconnect event reports a failure or time-out. A name is looked up
before the call returns, which can block briefly; pass an IP address when
that matters.

References: enDisconnect (handler), enPoll (handler)
*/
public handler enConnect(in pHost as Integer, in pRemoteHost as String, in pPort as Integer, in pChannels as Integer, in pData as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tPeer as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_connect(pHost, pRemoteHost, pPort, pChannels, pData) into tPeer
   end unsafe
   return tPeer
end handler

-- Polite disconnect: queued outbound traffic drains first, the far side is
-- told (it sees pData), and YOUR enetDisconnect event arrives via enPoll -
-- which is when the peer handle retires. Returns 0 or a negative error.
/**
Summary: Close a connection politely.

pPeer: The peer handle.

pData: A number the remote side receives in its enetDisconnect event.

Returns: 0, or a negative error.

Example:
get enDisconnect(sPeer, 0)

Description:
Use <enDisconnect> to end a connection cleanly. Packets already queued
are sent first and the remote side is told. Your own enetDisconnect event
arrives from <enPoll>, and the peer handle retires then.

References: enDisconnectNow (handler), enResetPeer (handler)
*/
public handler enDisconnect(in pPeer as Integer, in pData as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_disconnect(pPeer, pData) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- Abrupt disconnect: one last unsequenced notification to the far side, NO
-- local event - the handle retires immediately.
/**
Summary: Close a connection at once.

pPeer: The peer handle.

pData: A number the remote side receives in its enetDisconnect event.

Returns: 0, or a negative error.

Example:
get enDisconnectNow(sPeer, 0)

Description:
Use <enDisconnectNow> when you cannot wait for queued packets. One last
notice goes to the remote side, no local event follows, and the handle
retires immediately.

References: enDisconnect (handler), enResetPeer (handler)
*/
public handler enDisconnectNow(in pPeer as Integer, in pData as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_disconnect_now(pPeer, pData) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- Silent teardown: no notification either way, no event, immediate retire.
-- (The far side will time the connection out.)
/**
Summary: Drop a connection without telling either side.

pPeer: The peer handle.

Returns: 0, or a negative error.

Example:
get enResetPeer(sPeer)

Description:
Use <enResetPeer> to forget a connection silently. No notice is sent and
no event follows; the handle retires immediately and the remote side
times the connection out.

References: enDisconnect (handler), enDisconnectNow (handler)
*/
public handler enResetPeer(in pPeer as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_reset_peer(pPeer) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Public API: sending
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Queue pData (0..60000 bytes) to one peer on pChannel with pFlags (kSend*).
-- Delivery rides the next pump (enPoll) or an explicit enFlush. Returns 0,
-- or a negative error (-2 stale, -3 bad channel/flags, -4 over budget).
/**
Summary: Queue a packet of binary data to one peer.

pPeer: The peer handle.

pChannel: The channel, from 0 to one less than the channel count.

pData: The bytes to send, 0 to 60000 of them.

pFlags: 0 for reliable delivery, 1 for unreliable (sequenced, may be
dropped) or 2 for unsequenced (may be dropped or arrive out of order).

Returns: 0, or a negative error: -2 the peer is gone, -3 a bad channel or
flag, -4 more than 60000 bytes.

Example:
get enSend(sPeer, 0, textEncode("hello", "UTF-8"), 0)

Description:
Use <enSend> to send one message. It goes out on the next <enPoll> or
<enFlush>, and arrives whole, as one enetReceive event, or not at all.
Use 0 (reliable) unless the data is soon replaced, like a position
update.

References: enSendText (handler), enBroadcast (handler), enFlush (handler)
*/
public handler enSend(in pPeer as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pData as Data, in pFlags as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   variable tLen as Integer
   put the number of bytes in pData into tLen
   if tLen is 0 then
      -- a zero-length packet is legal; hand the shim the scratch pointer so
      -- there is a real (unread) address to pass
      _ensureScratch(kScratchCap)
      unsafe
         put _enx_send(pPeer, pChannel, sScratchPtr, 0, pFlags) into tR
      end unsafe
      return tR
   end if
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetBytePtr(pData) into tPtr
      put _enx_send(pPeer, pChannel, tPtr, tLen, pFlags) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- Text convenience: UTF-8 encode, then the same byte path as enSend. The far
-- side receives bytes; decode with textDecode(pEvent["payload"], "utf-8") in
-- LiveCode Script (or treat as bytes).
/**
Summary: Queue a packet of text to one peer.

pPeer: The peer handle.

pChannel: The channel, from 0 to one less than the channel count.

pText: The text to send, encoded as UTF-8.

pFlags: 0 reliable, 1 unreliable or 2 unsequenced, as for <enSend>.

Returns: 0, or a negative error, as for <enSend>.

Example:
get enSendText(sPeer, 0, "hello", 0)

Description:
Use <enSendText> to send text. It arrives as bytes: decode it with
textDecode(tEvent["payload"], "UTF-8") on the receiving side.

References: enSend (handler)
*/
public handler enSendText(in pPeer as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pText as String, in pFlags as Integer) returns Integer
   return enSend(pPeer, pChannel, _encodeText(pText), pFlags)
end handler

-- Queue one packet to EVERY connected peer of pHost (the server-fanout
-- primitive - one call, N deliveries). Same budget and flags as enSend.
/**
Summary: Queue a packet of binary data to every peer of a host.

pHost: The host handle.

pChannel: The channel.

pData: The bytes to send, 0 to 60000 of them.

pFlags: 0 reliable, 1 unreliable or 2 unsequenced, as for <enSend>.

Returns: 0, or a negative error, as for <enSend>.

Example:
get enBroadcast(sServer, 1, tStateData, 1)

Description:
Use <enBroadcast> on a server to send the same message to every
connected peer in one call.

References: enBroadcastText (handler), enSend (handler)
*/
public handler enBroadcast(in pHost as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pData as Data, in pFlags as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   variable tLen as Integer
   put the number of bytes in pData into tLen
   if tLen is 0 then
      _ensureScratch(kScratchCap)
      unsafe
         put _enx_broadcast(pHost, pChannel, sScratchPtr, 0, pFlags) into tR
      end unsafe
      return tR
   end if
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetBytePtr(pData) into tPtr
      put _enx_broadcast(pHost, pChannel, tPtr, tLen, pFlags) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

/**
Summary: Queue a packet of text to every peer of a host.

pHost: The host handle.

pChannel: The channel.

pText: The text to send, encoded as UTF-8.

pFlags: 0 reliable, 1 unreliable or 2 unsequenced, as for <enSend>.

Returns: 0, or a negative error, as for <enSend>.

Example:
get enBroadcastText(sServer, 0, "Game starts in 5 seconds", 0)

Description:
Use <enBroadcastText> on a server to send the same text to every
connected peer.

References: enBroadcast (handler), enSendText (handler)
*/
public handler enBroadcastText(in pHost as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pText as String, in pFlags as Integer) returns Integer
   return enBroadcast(pHost, pChannel, _encodeText(pText), pFlags)
end handler

-- Push queued packets onto the wire NOW without servicing inbound. Useful
-- right after a burst of sends when the next enPoll tick is milliseconds
-- away and latency matters.
/**
Summary: Send queued packets now.

pHost: The host handle.

Returns: 0, or a negative error.

Example:
get enSend(sPeer, 0, tData, 0)
get enFlush(sHost)

Description:
Use <enFlush> after a burst of sends when the next <enPoll> is too far
away. It sends without reading incoming traffic.

References: enPoll (handler), enSend (handler)
*/
public handler enFlush(in pHost as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_flush(pHost) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Public API: tuning
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Retransmission-timeout scaling: pLimit is a multiplier on the mean RTT
-- before escalation, bounded by pMinMs/pMaxMs (0 = ENet's default for each).
/**
Summary: Set how long a peer may go unanswered before it times out.

pPeer: The peer handle.

pLimit: How many times the average round trip to wait before giving up,
or 0 for ENet's default.

pMinMs: The shortest time-out in milliseconds, or 0 for ENet's default.

pMaxMs: The longest time-out in milliseconds, or 0 for ENet's default.

Returns: 0, or a negative error.

Example:
get enSetPeerTimeout(sPeer, 0, 5000, 30000)

Description:
Use <enSetPeerTimeout> to notice a lost connection sooner, or to be more
patient on a slow network.

References: enSetPeerPingInterval (handler)
*/
public handler enSetPeerTimeout(in pPeer as Integer, in pLimit as Integer, in pMinMs as Integer, in pMaxMs as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_set_peer_timeout(pPeer, pLimit, pMinMs, pMaxMs) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- How often ENet pings an idle peer (keeps RTT fresh and NATs open).
/**
Summary: Set how often an idle peer is pinged.

pPeer: The peer handle.

pMs: The interval in milliseconds.

Returns: 0, or a negative error.

Example:
get enSetPeerPingInterval(sPeer, 500)

Description:
Use <enSetPeerPingInterval> to keep round-trip times fresh and routers'
NAT entries open while nothing else is being sent.

References: enSetPeerTimeout (handler), enPeerStatus (handler)
*/
public handler enSetPeerPingInterval(in pPeer as Integer, in pMs as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_set_peer_ping_interval(pPeer, pMs) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- Throttle a host (bytes/second; 0 = unlimited) - ENet shapes its sends and
-- advertises the window to peers.
/**
Summary: Change a host's bandwidth limits.

pHost: The host handle.

pInBW: The incoming bandwidth in bytes per second, or 0 for unlimited.

pOutBW: The outgoing bandwidth in bytes per second, or 0 for unlimited.

Returns: 0, or a negative error.

Example:
get enSetHostBandwidth(sHost, 0, 64000)

Description:
Use <enSetHostBandwidth> to throttle a live host. ENet paces its own
sends and tells its peers the limit.

References: enHostCreateServer (handler), enHostCreateClient (handler)
*/
public handler enSetHostBandwidth(in pHost as Integer, in pInBW as Integer, in pOutBW as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _enx_set_host_bandwidth(pHost, pInBW, pOutBW) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Public API: status
-- ===================================================================== --

-- One live snapshot of a peer: keys host, peer, address, state (kPeerState*),
-- rtt (ms), packetLoss (0..1), packetsSent, packetsLost, bytesSent,
-- bytesReceived. {} for a stale handle (a retired peer is "gone", not an
-- error). ONE FFI round-trip.
/**
Summary: Report the state of one connection.

pPeer: The peer handle.

Returns: An array with the keys "host", "peer", "address", "state",
"rtt", "packetLoss", "packetsSent", "packetsLost", "bytesSent" and
"bytesReceived", or an empty array when the peer is gone.

Example:
local tStatus
put enPeerStatus(sPeer) into tStatus
if tStatus["state"] is 5 then put tStatus["rtt"] && "ms" into field "Ping"

Description:
Use <enPeerStatus> to show a connection's health. "state" is 5 when
connected (0 disconnected, 1 to 4 connecting, 6 to 8 disconnecting, 9
zombie), "rtt" is the round trip in milliseconds and "packetLoss" is from
0 to 1.

References: enHostStatus (handler)
*/
public handler enPeerStatus(in pPeer as Integer) returns Array
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tRec as Array
   put {} into tRec
   _ensureScratch(kScratchCap)
   unsafe
      put _enx_peer_status(pPeer, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
   end unsafe
   if tCount < 0 then
      _ensureScratch(-tCount)
      unsafe
         put _enx_peer_status(pPeer, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
      end unsafe
   end if
   if tCount <= 0 then
      return tRec
   end if
   return _parseRecord(_bufToData(sScratchPtr, tCount), 1)
end handler

-- One live snapshot of a host: keys host, address (the bound socket via
-- getsockname, so a port-0 server shows its real ephemeral port; an unbound
-- client host shows "0.0.0.0:0"), peerCount. {} for a stale handle.
/**
Summary: Report the state of a host.

pHost: The host handle.

Returns: An array with the keys "host", "address" and "peerCount", or an
empty array when the host is gone.

Example:
local tStatus
put enHostStatus(sServer) into tStatus
put tStatus["address"] into field "Listening on"

Description:
Use <enHostStatus> to find the address a host is bound to, including the
port the system chose for port 0, and how many peers it has. A client
host shows "0.0.0.0:0".

References: enPeerStatus (handler)
*/
public handler enHostStatus(in pHost as Integer) returns Array
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tRec as Array
   put {} into tRec
   _ensureScratch(kScratchCap)
   unsafe
      put _enx_host_status(pHost, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
   end unsafe
   if tCount < 0 then
      _ensureScratch(-tCount)
      unsafe
         put _enx_host_status(pHost, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tCount
      end unsafe
   end if
   if tCount <= 0 then
      return tRec
   end if
   return _parseRecord(_bufToData(sScratchPtr, tCount), 1)
end handler

-- ===================================================================== --
--  Public API: the drain (THE PUMP)
-- ===================================================================== --

-- Service pHost and return every pending event as a List of Arrays, each
-- with "code" (kEvent*), "name" (the semantic message: enetConnect,
-- enetDisconnect, enetReceive, enetError), and the event's fields (host,
-- peer, address, data / channel, payload / error). ONE FFI round-trip per
-- call (the single-thread playbook); the shim's one-slot stash + ENet's own
-- queue make any buffer size lossless and ordered, so a grow-and-retry here
-- is a rare, safe event, not a correctness worry.
--
-- PUMP OR NOTHING: call this on a timer even when you expect nothing -
-- connects, sends, retransmissions, and pings all progress inside it. The
-- helpers stack (enStartPolling) does it for you.
/**
Summary: Service a host and return its pending events.

pHost: The host handle.

Returns: A list of event arrays. Each has "code", "name" (enetConnect,
enetDisconnect, enetReceive or enetError) and the event's own keys:
"host", "peer", "address", "data", "channel", "payload" or "error".

Example:
local tEvents
put enPoll(sHost) into tEvents
repeat for each element tEvent in tEvents
   if tEvent["name"] is "enetReceive" then
      put textDecode(tEvent["payload"], "UTF-8") & return after field "Chat"
   end if
end repeat

Description:
Use <enPoll> on a timer for as long as a host lives: connecting, sending,
resending and receiving all happen inside it, and nothing happens without
it. Its interval sets the latency, 16 to 33 milliseconds for real-time
play or 100 or more for chat. Every pending event comes back in one call,
in order.

The enetHelpers library runs this loop for you with enStartPolling and
sends each event to your stack as a message of the same name.

References: enConnect (handler), enSend (handler), enFlush (handler)
*/
public handler enPoll(in pHost as Integer) returns List
   variable tEvents as List
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tData as Data
   variable tOffset as Integer
   variable tI as Integer
   variable tCode as Integer
   variable tBodyLen as Integer
   variable tEvent as Array
   variable tTries as Integer
   put [] into tEvents
   _ensureDrain(kDrainCap)
   put 0 into tTries
   repeat forever
      unsafe
         put _enx_poll(pHost, sDrainPtr, sDrainCap) into tCount
      end unsafe
      -- -needed is always far below the small error codes (-1..-6): the
      -- smallest possible entry needs more than a dozen bytes. Grow and
      -- retry; anything else negative is a real error -> empty list.
      if tCount < -6 and tTries < 4 then
         _ensureDrain(-tCount)
         add 1 to tTries
      else
         exit repeat
      end if
   end repeat
   if tCount <= 0 then
      return tEvents
   end if
   -- walk [count:u16] then count x [type:u16][bodyLen:u16][kvrecord]
   put _bufToData(sDrainPtr, sDrainCap) into tData
   put 3 into tOffset
   repeat with tI from 1 up to tCount
      put _readU16(tData, tOffset) into tCode
      add 2 to tOffset
      put _readU16(tData, tOffset) into tBodyLen
      add 2 to tOffset
      put _parseRecord(tData, tOffset) into tEvent
      put tCode into tEvent["code"]
      put _eventName(tCode) into tEvent["name"]
      push tEvent onto back of tEvents
      add tBodyLen to tOffset
   end repeat
   return tEvents
end handler

end library
